    "title": "Rising creatinine and declining eGFR on TDF",
    "message": "Guidelines require serum creatinine and eGFR monitoring for patients on TDF, and list chronic kidney disease and acute kidney injury as major TDF toxicities. Patient's creatinine has steadily risen from 0.9 (2020) to 1.4 (2026), with eGFR now 52 mL/min/1.73m², meeting Grade 2 renal toxicity (<60 mL/min). TDF is a likely contributor given the progressive decline over 3 years on therapy. Urinalysis showing protein 1+ further supports renal tubular involvement. Against drug attribution: no diabetes or hypertension, stable weight, and no oedema. However, the temporal relationship and proteinuria strengthen TDF nephrotoxicity as the leading explanation. Occasional ibuprofen use (2-3x/week) may compound renal risk.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Do LFTs and RFTs as per TDF toxicity management. Consider switching TDF to TAF or alternative backbone given eGFR <60 and progressive creatinine rise. Minimize NSAID use.",
